      "text": "To find Margaret's mean score, we first need to determine the sum of Cyprian's scores. We know that the mean score of Cyprian's three test scores is 90. The mean score is calculated by summing all the scores and dividing by the number of scores. Therefore, we can set up the following equation for Cyprian's scores:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Mean score of Cyprian's scores} = \\frac{\\text{Sum of Cyprian's scores}}{\\text{Number of Cyprian's scores}}\n\\]\n\nGiven that the mean score of Cyprian's scores is 90 and there are 3 scores, we can write:\n\n\\[\n90 = \\frac{\\text{Sum of Cyprian's scores}}{3}\n\\]\n\nTo find the sum of Cyprian's scores, we multiply both sides of the equation by 3:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of Cyprian's scores} = 90 \\times 3 = 270\n\\]\n\nSo, the sum of Cyprian's scores is 270. We are given the scores 85, 87, and 92. Let's denote the third score as \\( x \\). The sum of all five scores is:\n\n\\[\n85 + 87 + 92 + x + 94 + 98 = 270\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\n466 + x = 270\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we subtract 466 from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx = 270 - 466 = -206\n\\]\n\nHowever, we notice that we made an error in the interpretation of the problem. The correct approach is to find the sum of the remaining two scores (93 and 98) and subtract from the total sum we already calculated. So, the sum of the remaining two scores is:\n\n\\[\n93 + 98 = 191\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the scores Cyprian has is:\n\n\\[\n270 - 191 = 79\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the mean score of Margaret's three scores. Let the scores be 85, 87, and 94 (as the others scored 92 and 98 as Cyprian's scores are 85, 87, and 93). The mean score of Margaret's scores is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Mean score of Margaret's scores} = \\frac{85 + 87 + 94}{3} = \\frac{266}{3} = 88.67\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Margaret's mean score is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{88.67}\n\\]",
